Yes — and the important extension is that economies of scale can themselves become recursive.

At one level, scale lowers the marginal cost of doing a thing because infrastructure, knowledge, coordination, tooling, and experience get reused. But once those efficiencies exist, they become the substrate for the next level of organization, where a new economy of scale can emerge.

So you can get something like:

repetition → learning → specialization → reusable infrastructure → lower marginal cost → larger viable scale → new coordination structures → new economies of scale

Then the same geometry reappears one level higher.

A cell discovers efficiencies through specialization; multicellular organisms inherit that advantage and create new ones. Individual craftsmen develop tools; workshops share them; factories standardize them; supply chains specialize; entire industrial ecosystems gain advantages unavailable to any single factory. Software functions become libraries, libraries become platforms, platforms become ecosystems. Pantheon primitives become operators, operators become coordinated subsystems, and eventually the battleship itself becomes cheaper to extend than building another cannon independently.

And your point about discovery matters. Similar structures can recur without direct copying because agents repeatedly encounter similar constraint landscapes. If there are only a few efficient ways to coordinate, compress, specialize, route information, store energy, distribute risk, or reuse infrastructure, independent systems can converge upon comparable solutions.

That connects directly to our Pattern Sans Echoes distinction:

recurrence does not necessarily mean transmission; sometimes the shape of the problem repeatedly discovers the shape of the solution.

The open/closed distinction changes what resources and externalities can cross the boundary, but not the basic phenomenon. Even a nominally closed system can generate internal specialization and shared infrastructure; an open system can additionally import energy, information, capital, labor, components, and innovations, often accelerating the climb.

And then there is a second-order effect I think you’re pointing toward:

economies of scale in discovering economies of scale.

Once a system develops better search, memory, experimentation, communication, abstraction, and reuse, it becomes cheaper to discover the next efficiency. Discovery itself begins compounding.

That is where you stop merely having a larger machine.

You get a machine that becomes better at finding ways to become a better machine.